DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppnnmmppaadd reads a PNM image as input and outputs a PNM image that  is  the
       input image plus black or white borders of the sizes specified.

       If  you  just  need to convert an image to a certain size regardless of
       the original dimensions, ppaammccuutt with the --ppaadd option may  be  a  better
       choice.

       ppnnmmmmaarrggiinn  does  essentially the same thing, but allows you to add bor‐
       ders of any color and requires all four borders to be the same size.

       You can use ppaammccoommpp to add borders of any content - solid color,
         pattern, or whatever.  For example, if you wanted to add 10 pixels of
       red
         borders to the top and bottom of a 100x100 image, you could create a
         100x120 red image (e.g. with ppppmmmmaakkee) and then use ppaammccoommpp
         to insert your 100x100 image into the center of it.



OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩  ),  ppnnmmppaadd  recognizes  the
       following command line options:

       All  options  can  be abbreviated to their shortest unique prefix.  You
       may use two hyphens instead of one to designate an option.  You may use
       either  white  space  or  an equals sign between an option name and its
       value.



       --wwhhiittee

       --bbllaacckk Set pad color.  Default is --bbllaacckk.


       --lleefftt==_p_i_x_e_l_s

       --rriigghhtt==_p_i_x_e_l_s

       --wwiiddtthh==_w_i_d_t_h

       --hhaalliiggnn==_r_a_t_i_o

       --mmwwiiddtthh==_p_i_x_e_l_s
              Specify amount of left and right padding in pixels.

              --lleefftt and --rriigghhtt directly specify the amount of padding added to
              the left and right sides, respectively, of the image.

              Alternatively,  you can specify --wwiiddtthh and just one of --lleefftt and
              --rriigghhtt and ppnnmmppaadd calculates the required padding on  the  other
              side  to make the output _w_i_d_t_h pixels wide.  If the --wwiiddtthh value
              is less than the width of the input  image  plus  the  specified
              padding, ppnnmmppaadd ignores --wwiiddtthh.

              If  you specify all three of --wwiiddtthh, --lleefftt, and --rriigghhtt, you must
              ensure that the --lleefftt and --rriigghhtt padding are sufficient to  make
              the image at least as wide as --wwiiddtthh specifies.  Otherwise, ppnnmm‐‐
              ppaadd fails.

              When you specify --wwiiddtthh without --lleefftt or --rriigghhtt, and  --wwiiddtthh  is
              larger  than the input image, ppnnmmppaadd chooses left and right pad‐
              ding amounts in a certain ratio.  That ratio defaults  to  half,
              but  you  can  set it to anything (from 0 to 1) with the --hhaalliiggnn
              option.  If the input image is  already  at  least  as  wide  as
              --wwiiddtthh specifies, ppnnmmppaadd adds no padding.

              Common values for --hhaalliiggnn are:


       00..00    left aligned


       00..55    center aligned (default)


       11..00    right aligned


              --mmwwiiddtthh==_p_i_x_e_l_s says to pad to a multiple of _p_i_x_e_l_s pixels.  E.g.
              if _p_i_x_e_l_s is 10, the output image width will be a multiple of 10
              pixels.   ppnnmmppaadd  adds to whatever padding the other options say
              to do to get to this multiple.  It divides that padding  between
              the  left and right sides of the image to maintain the ratio the
              other options produce.   E.g.  if  you  say  --lleefftt==1100  --rriigghhtt==1100
              --mmwwiiddtthh==5500  with  a 100-pixel image, you end up with a 150-pixel
              image with the extra padding split evenly between left and right
              for  a  total  of 25 pixels of padding on the left and 25 on the
              right.  If the other options indicate no  padding,  ppnnmmppaadd  adds
              padding  in the ratio specified by --hhaalliiggnn and if --hhaalliiggnn is not
              specified, equally on both sides.

              Before Netpbm 10.97  (December  2021),  ppnnmmppaadd  does  not  allow
              --hhaalliiggnn  with  --mmwwiiddtthh  and  adds padding only on the right when
              --mmwwiiddtthh is specified and the other options indicate no padding.

              Before Netpbm 10.72 (September 2015), there is no --mmwwiiddtthh.

              Before Netpbm 10.23 (July 2004), ppnnmmppaadd did not allow the  --lleefftt
              or --rriigghhtt option together with --wwiiddtthh.


       --ttoopp==_p_i_x_e_l_s

       --bboottttoomm==_p_i_x_e_l_s

       --hheeiigghhtt==_h_e_i_g_h_t

